{
 "id": "2023-yan-wood",
 "name": "Wood Coverage in Activity Rooms for Older Adults: Semantic Differential",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Yan & Guo (2023), Buildings 13:2086. Participants first see the activity room of an old-age building without wood (the paper's control picture), then the six scene sets of the paper's Table 1 in random order: 15%, 30%, 45%, 60% and 75% wall wood coverage (three layouts each) and 90% (two layouts). Each set is rated on the paper's nine semantic differential pairs (succinct–sophisticated, quiet–impetuous, natural–artificial, familiar–offish, warm–cool, bright–dim, secure–insecure, discernible–indiscernible, aesthetic–drab), 7 levels from +3 to −3 with the first, positive adjective at +3. Gender and age are asked first. Stimuli: the paper's own 18 renderings (SketchUp 2019 + Enscape 2.0), cropped from Table 1 of the CC BY 4.0 article; credited on the page and removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper showed each set for 5 minutes on a hemispherical screen in a lab, after 15 minutes of rest and an activity-room sound recording, and split the six sets over three sessions at least 24 hours apart; here all sets are rated in one online session and viewing time is free (recorded); (2) heart rate and blood pressure are not measured; (3) the images come from the published table (about 485 × 255 px); (4) the paper does not say which end of each pair is +3; the template puts the positive adjective (listed first in the paper) at +3.",
